 ### `dori send <slack|telegram|discord> --to TARGET --text TEXT [--thread ID] [--edit ID]`
 Posts a message, or edits one with `--edit`. Tokens come from `DORI_SLACK_TOKEN`, or when that is unset or empty from the file `DORI_SLACK_TOKEN_FILE` names (plus `DORI_SLACK_COOKIE` for a user token). The file must be a regular file you own and owner-only (`chmod 600`), checked and read through one open descriptor; a file open to group or others, owned by someone else, not a regular file (a FIFO never blocks the read), missing or empty is refused. Windows has no mode bits or uid to check, so there the file's ACL is the owner's to set, and `dori boot` prints `SLACK token-file: the token file's NTFS ACL is not checked on Windows; ...` as a reminder. Other tokens come from `DORI_TELEGRAM_TOKEN` or `DORI_DISCORD_TOKEN`. Text containing `$(` is refused, since it can only come from a shell string. Rate limits are retried with the server's wait time; other errors fail at once.
 
+### `dori send mattermost (--thread mattermost:CHANNEL:ROOT | --to CHANNEL [--thread ROOT]) --text TEXT [--file PATH ...]`, `dori send mattermost --edit POST --text TEXT`
+Posts to a channel, replies in a thread, attaches files (each uploaded to the channel first), or edits a post the Dori wrote. Prints `SENT <post-id> mattermost:<channel>:<root>`; for a new top-level post the root is the post itself, so that reference is the new thread's. Needs `DORI_MATTERMOST_URL` and `DORI_MATTERMOST_TOKEN`, or the file `DORI_MATTERMOST_TOKEN_FILE` names. Every id must be a 26-character Mattermost id, and text containing `$(` is refused. A channel or root id listed in `DORI_MATTERMOST_BLOCKED` (comma-separated) is never written to: the command exits 3 before any request, for a retired thread or a channel the owner closed. The same list guards `thread`.
+
+As hooks: `"threadReply": ["dori", "send", "mattermost", "--thread", "{thread}", "--text", "{text}"]` and `"threadDone": ["dori", "thread", "mattermost", "{thread}", "--status", "done", "--note", "{text}"]`, with lanes launched with `--thread mattermost:<channel>:<root>`.
+
+### `dori thread mattermost <REF> --status working|waiting|done [--note TEXT]`
+Mattermost threads have no name, so a thread's state lives on its status line, the way Telegram and Discord name their topic or thread: the root post when the Dori wrote it, else the Dori's first reply in the thread, which gets a `[working]`, `[waiting]` or `[done]` prefix (a previous prefix is replaced). With no Dori post in the thread yet, the note (or the bare state) is posted as the status line. `--note` is then posted as a reply. `done` is the archive mark, and `working` reopens the thread when the owner writes in it again. Prints `THREAD mattermost <ref> <state> status=<post-id>`.
+
+### `dori typing mattermost (--thread REF | --to CHANNEL [--thread ROOT])`
+Shows "Dori is typing" in the channel or thread until stopped, refreshed every 4 seconds. A wrong target or token fails on the first call, before `TYPING_READY` is printed. Run it in the background while an answer is written and stop it when the answer is posted.
+
+### `dori download mattermost <file-id> [--to DIR]`
+Saves an inbound file (the ids `inbound` prints after `files=`) to `DIR`, by default `<stateDir>/files`, and prints `FILE <path>`. A voice note goes on to `dori transcribe <path>`.
+
 ### `dori send gateway --text TEXT [--edit ID]`, `--status ...`, `--question ...`, `--receipt ...`
 The dori.bot surface on a Dori VM: post, edit in place, a status row with work rows, a question with options, a receipt. It writes to the gateway's local socket and needs no token. A send the gateway cannot take yet is retried with the same `op_id`; when the backoff is spent it exits non-zero with `gateway_unavailable`. Frames, flags and printed lines: `gateway-socket.md`. The rules for this surface, including when to ask the user before acting, are in `../rules/dori-bot.md`.
 
-### `dori presence <slack|discord>`
+### `dori presence <slack|discord|mattermost>`
 Keeps the account shown as online until stopped.
 - **Discord:** the bot connects to the gateway and identifies as online.
+- **Mattermost:** the account's status is set to online every minute, so an auto-away never sticks.
 - **Slack, user token:** it opens one web-client-type socket and tickles it every minute. Slack shows a user active only while such a socket is open, and auto-aways an idle one after about 30 minutes.
 - Run one instance per account. Two writers flip each other's presence.
 
